From 87a30f05258aeb1d98aacd0b0e844b781975a469 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 09:02:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(dashboard): thread lanes keep /api/status answering when
 slow reads fill the pool [daveey]

On one occasion the hub's always-on backend spent ~2 h with AnyIO's shared
40-thread pool full of session and kanban reads waiting on locks
(session-panel /changes up to 926 s, /api/sessions 627 s). /api/status, which
makes ~10 run_in_threadpool calls, queued behind them for up to 632 s; Desktop
timed out (30 s) on every hub call and retried, and the backend ran every
abandoned read to completion.

hermes_cli/web_thread_lanes.py gives route groups their own thread budget:
- control (4): /api/status thread work, the sidebar cache-hit lookup.
- sessions (8): /api/sessions, /api/profiles/sessions, the sidebar and
  projects-tree misses, /api/session-kanban-tasks.
- probe (2, 2 s deadline in /api/status): the state.db reads inside
  /api/status (storage probe, active-session count, FTS status). A busy probe
  lane leaves those fields degraded/0 instead of holding the answer.
- feed (6): for plugin catch-up reads (hermes-mods session-panel, user-steps).
A caller waits at most dashboard.thread_lanes.queue_deadline_s (25 s, below
Desktop's 30 s) for a lane thread, or until its client disconnects; then it
gets 503 + Retry-After and the work never starts. Started work runs to the
end. Lane threads never use the shared pool. Sizes and the deadline come from
config.yaml, read once at start-up off the loop. /api/health reports per-lane
counters (in_use, waiting, served, rejected, disconnected, max_wait_s).

Tests: unit tests for waiting, the deadline (503, never run), disconnects,
lane isolation, independence from the shared pool, config; a load test that
fills the shared pool and the sessions lane through the real routes and
requires the real get_status() and /api/health to stay fast. It fails on
main's routes ("/api/status is stuck behind the slow reads"). Each guard was
mutation-checked. test_profile_scan_http_admission now asserts scans take 0
shared-pool threads (was 1) and run on the sessions lane.

Co-Authored-By: Contributor <contributor@example.com>
